# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Environment template for the PLAYWRIGHT E2E STACK ONLY.
#
#   cp .env.e2e.example .env.e2e        (Linux / macOS / Git Bash)
#   Copy-Item .env.e2e.example .env.e2e (PowerShell)
#
# This is deliberately SEPARATE from `.env`. The E2E stack is a throwaway,
# self-contained environment that must boot on a machine that has never had this
# app configured — no Gmail app password, no Twilio account, no OAuth client
# registrations, no Aiven instance. Every value below is a working local default,
# and none of them is a real secret, which is why (unlike `.env`) an `.env.e2e`
# built from this file is safe on any developer's machine and in CI.
#
# Used by:  docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.e2e.yml \
#             --env-file .env.e2e up --build
# which `npm run e2e` does for you (see package.json / playwright.config.ts).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── MySQL ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A throwaway database in a throwaway container. `docker compose down -v` between
# runs is what makes the suite reproducible — see the E2E README.
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=e2e-root
MYSQL_DATABASE=tessera_e2e
MYSQL_USERNAME=root
MYSQL_PASSWORD=e2e-root
# Host port for the MySQL container. Deliberately NOT 3306: a developer running
# this almost certainly has a local MySQL already bound there, and a port clash
# fails as a confusing app-side connection error rather than an obvious one.
MYSQL_HOST_PORT=3307

# ── Spring ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `dev` is REQUIRED, not a convenience: DemoDataSeeder is @Profile("dev"), so on
# any other profile the stack comes up with ZERO login accounts and every auth
# test fails with nothing to sign in as. docker-compose.yml defaults to `prod`,
# which is why docker-compose.e2e.yml overrides it.
SPRING_ACTIVE_PROFILES=dev
CONTAINER_PORT=8080
APP_PORT=8090

# ── JWT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Throwaway signing key (>= 32 chars). Regenerate with: openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=ZTJlLW9ubHktand0LXNpZ25pbmctc2VjcmV0LW5vdC1hLXJlYWwtc2VjcmV0LTAxMjM0NTY3ODk=

# ── Per-organization SSO ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Must decode to EXACTLY 32 bytes or EncryptionUtil fails fast at startup and the
# whole stack never becomes healthy. Regenerate with: openssl rand -base64 32
ORG_IDP_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=ZTJlLWxvY2FsLXRlc3Rpbmcta2V5LW5vdC1hLXNlY3I=

# ── Email ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Intentionally non-functional. Nothing in the E2E suite depends on an email
# actually being delivered, and every seeded account is @tessera.dev, which
# EmailServiceImpl#isSuppressedRecipient drops before dispatch anyway.
#
# A pointer at nothing was still enough to wedge the whole suite once: because
# spring-boot-starter-mail is on the classpath, Spring Boot auto-registered a
# MailHealthIndicator that dialled this host on every /actuator/health probe.
# The app booted fine and served / with a 200, but health stayed DOWN forever,
# so Docker's healthcheck and Playwright's `webServer` gate never opened. The
# fix is NOT to point this at a real mailbox or run a mail-catcher sidecar —
# it is `management.health.mail.enabled: false` in application.yml, which is
# also the correct production setting (an SMTP outage should not make the ALB
# drain a healthy instance). See the comment on that property.
MAIL_USERNAME=e2e@example.invalid
MAIL_PASSWORD=not-a-real-app-password
MAIL_HOST=localhost
MAIL_PORT=2525

# ── Frontend ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The compose stack is single-origin: the Angular bundle is baked into the Spring
# Boot jar's static resources (see Dockerfile stage 2), so the SPA and the API are
# the same host:port and CORS never enters the picture.
UI_APP_URL=http://localhost:8090

# ── Profile image storage ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
IMAGE_STORAGE_TYPE=local
IMAGE_STORAGE_PATH=/app/data/images

# ── SMS 2FA ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Left as placeholders on purpose: TwilioVerifyUtils logs the code instead of
# sending it when these are not real, so no Twilio account (and no per-message
# cost) is needed to run the suite.
TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=+10000000000
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=ACxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=e2e-not-a-real-token
TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID=VAxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# ── Login-anomaly detection (THE critical E2E setting) ───────────────────────
# MUST be false for the suite to be able to log in at all. The chain that makes
# this non-optional:
#
#   1. DemoDataSeeder writes LOGIN_ATTEMPT_SUCCESS rows for every seeded account
#      with a fixed device ("Chrome on macOS") and IP (10.0.1.x), so no seeded
#      account is a "no history" account — LoginRiskServiceImpl's never-flag-the-
#      first-login escape hatch does not apply.
#   2. A Playwright browser is a different device string AND a different network
#      prefix, so BOTH NEW_DEVICE and NEW_NETWORK fire and the sign-in escalates
#      to email step-up.
#   3. Step-up mails the one-time code to a @tessera.dev address, which
#      EmailServiceImpl#isSuppressedRecipient silently drops.
#
# Net effect with detection on: the login can never complete and never times out
# into a useful error — it just sits on the MFA screen forever. application.yml's
# own comment on this flag anticipates exactly this case ("useful when demoing the
# app from a fresh network where every sign-in would legitimately look new").
#
# This disables ONLY the risk-based step-up escalation. Enrolled TOTP/SMS 2FA,
# password hashing, JWT validation, and every authorization rule are untouched, so
# the suite still exercises the real security path.
ANOMALY_DETECTION_ENABLED=false

# ── Rate limiting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Raised for the E2E stack only. The shipped defaults are 10/min on auth paths
# and 200/min everywhere else, per IP (see `security.rate-limit` in
# application.yml), and those defaults are correct — but the suite is a false
# positive for them.
#
# Inside Docker, every request the Playwright runner makes arrives at the app
# from a single bridge-gateway address, so the whole host collapses into one
# bucket. A fourteen-test run plus Playwright's once-per-second health polling
# is therefore, from the filter's point of view, one client issuing a sustained
# burst — exactly the shape the limiter exists to reject. It duly rejected it:
# the first green-stack run had six failures, four of them a bare 429 on an
# endpoint the test wasn't even about, and the other two downstream of the same
# thing (a missing ETag, and a login form that never appeared).
#
# The control is NOT simply switched off here. e2e/rate-limit.spec.ts drives the
# auth tier past this raised ceiling on purpose and asserts the 429, the
# Retry-After header and the HttpResponse envelope, so the limiter has more
# end-to-end coverage with these values than it had without them.
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_CAPACITY=120
RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL_CAPACITY=5000
